#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# r_solve

#% text
# `r_solve(eqs, a, n)` solves a linear recurrence with constant coefficients for the sequence `a(n)`, giving `[[a -> (n => sol)]]`; `r_solve(eqs, a(n), n)` gives `[[a(n) -> sol]]`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The recurrence is `sum c_k a(n + k) == 0` with constant `c_k` (shifts may be negative); the solution is a combination of `r^n` (and `n^j r^n` for a multiple root) over the roots of the characteristic polynomial.

#% item
# The constants are `c(1)`, `c(2)`, ...; initial values `a(k) == v` in `eqs` fix them.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Doubling:

#% cell
r_solve(a(n + 1) == 2*a(n), a(n), n)
#% output
#= [[a(n) -> c(1)*2^n]]

#% text
# The Fibonacci numbers:

#% cell
r_solve([a(n + 2) == a(n + 1) + a(n), a(0) == 0, a(1) == 1], a(n), n)
#% output
#= [[a(n) -> (-1/5)*(-(1 + 5^(1/2))^n + (1 - 5^(1/2))^n)*(1/2)^n*5^(1/2)]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A double root:

#% cell
r_solve(a(n + 2) - 4*a(n + 1) + 4*a(n) == 0, a(n), n)
#% output
#= [[a(n) -> c(1)*2^n + n*c(2)*2^n]]

#% text
# Shifts backwards:

#% cell
r_solve([a(n) == 3*a(n - 1), a(0) == 2], a, n)
#% output
#= [[a -> (n => 2*3^n)]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The tenth Fibonacci number from the closed form:

#% cell
expand(r_solve_value([a(n + 2) == a(n + 1) + a(n), a(0) == 0, a(1) == 1], a(n), n) /. n -> 10)
#% output
#= 55

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `r_solve_value`, `d_solve`, `fibonacci`
